    "lawCode": "Amd §94-c, Exec L",
    "lawSection": "Executive Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S6404\n\nSPONSOR: HINCHEY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the executive law, in relation to directing the office\nof renewable energy siting, in consultation with the public service\ncommission, to create a statewide map of all proposed major renewable\nenergy facility sites and an informational tab that calculates regional\nimpacts of such proposed sites\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to direct the Office of Renewable Energy\nSiting and the Public Service Commission to create a publicly available\nmap of all approved and proposed renewable energy generation projects\nacross New. York State in order to understand all regional impacts and\nprovide real-time tracking of the total amount of solar energy gener-\nation proposed.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one directs the Office of Renewable Energy Siting, in consulta-\ntion with the Public. Service Commission, to create and post on its\nwebsite a continuously updated map of all proposed renewable energy\ngeneration projects being reviewed through the Articles 10 or 94-C proc-\nesses in conjunction with all previously approved projects. This map\nwill be divided into the 10 regions used by the Regional Economic Devel-\nopment Councils to understand the regional impacts of renewable energy\ndevelopments state-wide. The map will include the approximate size\n(acreage) of the project, what the proposed generation capacity of each\nproject's application is, and clearly show the boundaries of the project\nin the municipality where the project is sited.\n\nAdditionally, an informational tab will accompany this map and include\neach project's total acreage, the acreage of the project being sited on\nprime agricultural land, the acreage of the project being sited on open\nspace, and the acreage of the project being sited on forested lands. The\nprojects will be organized according to the 10 regions used by the\nRegional Economic Development Council.\n\nSection two states the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nRural communities face many challenges - they're often economically\ndisadvantaged with fewer municipal resources - and are frequently\ntargeted for fast-tracked solar installations. While we must ensure that\nthe goals set forth in the CLCPA are met, we must also ensure the\nprotection of our rural communities and natural resources from thought-\nless overdevelopment Monitoring and tracking regional impacts are two of\nthe ways we can meet our climate goals while protecting disadvantaged,\nupstate communities from predatory practices.\n\nIn order to do this, we must have a comprehensive understanding of solar\nand other renewable energy developments state-wide at every step of\ntheir application process, from the notice of intent, through a permit-\nted application. The State must also track the types of land sited for\n\ndevelopment - such as farmland, foresdand, and open space - to under-\nstand true regional impacts.\n\nCurrently, there is no state-wide map tracking where renewable energy\ngenerating facilities are proposed to be developed. This creates an\ninformation gap where the public and State leaders do not know where\nprojects will be located, what types of land the project will be devel-\noped on, and what the adverse impacts are to site large-scale develop-\nments on these lands. Without a map and accompanying data, it is impos-\nsible to determine the true and impending adverse impacts on our\nstatewide food supply, our finite natural resources, and the communities\nthat are set to host them.\n\nThis map will further allow for real-time tracking of the total amount\nof solar energy generation being proposed and help make sure that we are\non track to meet these critical goals. We cannot reliably know that our\nCLCPA goals are on track to be met if the State entities that are tasked\nwith considering and approving these projects are not tracking the\nholistic landscape of projects both approved and proposed. With this map\nand accompanying informational tab, the public and State leaders will\nhave an understanding of all solar projects at every stage of their\ndevelopment within New York State.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the sixtieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law; provided, however, that the amendments to paragraph (c) of\nsubdivision 5 of section 94-c of the executive law made by section one\nof this act shall not affect the repeal of such section and shall be\ndeemed repealed therewith. Effective immediately, the addition, amend-\nment and/or repeal of any rule or regulation necessary for the implemen-\ntation of this act on its effective date are authorized to be made and\ncompleted on or before such effective date.\n",
